The app runs as a single Node.js process. Log of two requests that overlap:

08:14:02.101 [req-a] guard     userId=u_17
08:14:02.103 [req-b] guard     userId=u_42
08:14:02.140 [req-a] markDone  user=u_42 lesson=lsn_3
08:14:02.151 [req-b] markDone  user=u_42 lesson=lsn_8

Statement: "Request req-a could only have read u_42 if the two requests ran on different threads; in one Node.js process, a field set at the start of a request stays the same until that request's own code changes it."

The fix makes the context request-scoped. Nothing else changes:

@Injectable({ scope: Scope.REQUEST })
export class RequestContext {
  constructor(@Inject(REQUEST) private readonly req: Request) {}
  get userId() { return this.req.user.id; }
}

@Injectable()   // default scope
export class ProgressService {
  constructor(private readonly ctx: RequestContext, private readonly repo: ProgressRepository) {
    console.log('ProgressService created');
  }
}

@Controller('progress')
export class ProgressController {
  constructor(private readonly progress: ProgressService) {}
}

The app starts, then receives three requests to POST /progress/lessons/:id/complete.

Statement: "After the three requests, the line 'ProgressService created' appears in the log exactly once, because ProgressService itself is still declared with the default scope."

NotificationsService batches "lesson completed" emails and sends them every 30 seconds. To lose nothing on deploy, it flushes on shutdown:

@Injectable()
export class NotificationsService implements OnApplicationShutdown {
  async onApplicationShutdown(signal?: string) {
    const sent = await this.flush();
    this.logger.log(`flushed ${sent} pending notifications (${signal})`);
  }
}

// main.ts
const app = await NestFactory.create(AppModule);
await app.listen(3000);

Deploy log of the old container:

10:02:11 Received SIGTERM
10:02:11 container exited with code 143

The "flushed ... pending notifications" line is missing, and learners report missing emails for lessons completed just before 10:02.

Statement: "The flush never ran because main.ts does not call app.enableShutdownHooks(), so Nest did not listen for SIGTERM and never called onApplicationShutdown."
